WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.178 --> 00:00:03.511
(soothing guitar music)

00:00:06.940 --> 00:00:09.310
- Climate change is a human story.

00:00:09.310 --> 00:00:11.540
The causes of climate change are man made

00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:13.910
and the solutions must be man made.

00:00:13.910 --> 00:00:15.460
How much of the landscape--

00:00:15.460 --> 00:00:18.010
In order to reduce climate change,

00:00:18.010 --> 00:00:20.200
in order to adapt to these changes

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:23.010
and to mitigate our impact on the planet,

00:00:23.010 --> 00:00:25.383
we have to start with human stories.

00:00:26.610 --> 00:00:28.993
I love listening to people's stories.

00:00:30.250 --> 00:00:31.370
I'm Victoria Herrmann.

00:00:31.370 --> 00:00:34.393
I'm a geographer and a
National Geographic explorer.

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:39.700
When people read stories
about climate change,

00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:43.240
it's often in far off
places like the Arctic

00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:45.350
or small islands,

00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:47.873
but climate change affects everyone.

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:52.270
By the end of this century,

00:00:52.270 --> 00:00:55.640
at least 420 towns, cities and villages

00:00:55.640 --> 00:00:58.940
across the United States
will be partially underwater

00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:02.543
no matter how much we reduce
our greenhouse gases today.

00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:07.980
But, there's never a narrative
that should be hopeless.

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:10.650
There is hope in every
climate change story.

00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:12.963
It's just about finding
the right solutions.

00:01:14.260 --> 00:01:17.020
Over the course of 2016 and 2017,

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:20.270
I traveled across the United
States and U.S. Territories

00:01:20.270 --> 00:01:24.010
and conducted over 350
interviews with local leaders

00:01:24.010 --> 00:01:27.160
from the Chesapeake Bay to American Samoa,

00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:29.923
from Alabama to Alaska.

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:33.177
At first I thought the biggest challenges

00:01:33.177 --> 00:01:35.710
were going to be the loss of property,

00:01:35.710 --> 00:01:39.540
the loss of houses, of
critical infrastructure,

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:42.350
but what people really
wanted to talk to me about

00:01:42.350 --> 00:01:43.620
was losing histories,

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:45.600
the traditions that they can pass down

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:47.377
to the next generation.

00:01:47.377 --> 00:01:50.210
(soothing guitar music)

00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:52.550
I think of myself as first a listener

00:01:52.550 --> 00:01:54.020
and then a connector.

00:01:54.020 --> 00:01:55.028
- Oh, yes, absolutely.

00:01:55.028 --> 00:01:56.403
Like people, a lot of people that have--

00:01:56.403 --> 00:01:57.900
- [Victoria] Most days for me

00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:00.120
are coming to places like here.

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:02.650
We are on the Eastern shore
of the Chesapeake Bay.

00:02:02.650 --> 00:02:05.610
Geographically we're in a
place called Poverty Point

00:02:05.610 --> 00:02:07.850
but the great organization
we're working with

00:02:07.850 --> 00:02:09.470
has renamed it Patriot Point

00:02:09.470 --> 00:02:12.373
to reflect the work
they're doing with this.

00:02:12.373 --> 00:02:15.810
This is an incredible property
where vets come to hunt,

00:02:15.810 --> 00:02:18.900
to fish, to seek the
healing that they need.

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:20.410
- These are the coats that we give out

00:02:20.410 --> 00:02:22.983
to one of the service
members when they come in.

00:02:23.850 --> 00:02:25.145
- [Victoria] The owners of Patriot Point

00:02:25.145 --> 00:02:27.240
didn't realize that climate change

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:31.203
would impact them so
quickly and so intensely.

00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:35.890
I want to understand their history,

00:02:35.890 --> 00:02:38.853
their landscape and their
climate change challenges.

00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:42.513
So when you see shoreline erosion,

00:02:43.530 --> 00:02:45.680
is it usually with a big
storm that's coming in

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:47.201
or is it just a king tide?

00:02:47.201 --> 00:02:49.360
- We've had a lot this year.

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:52.503
We're having two to three
foot above normal tides.

00:02:53.730 --> 00:02:56.000
- [Victoria] When we
think of sea level rise

00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:57.760
and extreme storms,

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:00.270
we first go to our shorelines,

00:03:00.270 --> 00:03:02.740
to the Jersey shore with Hurricane Sandy,

00:03:02.740 --> 00:03:05.620
or to Louisiana with Hurricane Katrina,

00:03:05.620 --> 00:03:08.740
but climate change impacts
all bodies of water,

00:03:08.740 --> 00:03:11.480
whether they're bays that
can see sea level rise

00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:15.453
or rivers that can see flooding
into the plains around them.

00:03:18.070 --> 00:03:21.581
The Chesapeake Bay is one of
the fastest changing ecosystems

00:03:21.581 --> 00:03:24.793
in the United States
related to climate change.

00:03:26.470 --> 00:03:30.180
At Patriot Point, I walked
along the eroding edges

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:33.250
to see how the land has
changed over the past year,

00:03:33.250 --> 00:03:35.723
decade, maybe 50 years.

00:03:37.460 --> 00:03:39.650
We're looking for trees falling over,

00:03:39.650 --> 00:03:42.160
for tides rising above the marsh land,

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:44.290
to understand the rate of erosion

00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:47.022
and how much sea levels are rising.

00:03:47.022 --> 00:03:49.710
(soothing instrumental music)

00:03:49.710 --> 00:03:51.680
To get a different perspective on that,

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:54.830
we jumped onto a boat and
saw it from the water side

00:03:54.830 --> 00:03:58.370
so we can see how those waves
are cutting under the land,

00:03:58.370 --> 00:04:01.570
and seeing how that is
making this whole property

00:04:01.570 --> 00:04:03.763
more vulnerable to sea level rise.

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:08.520
- [Older Man] Now that was
the tidal pond right there.

00:04:08.520 --> 00:04:10.680
- [Victoria] Oh, wow, it's all filled in.

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:12.743
- And we're losin' a little bit there.

00:04:13.730 --> 00:04:16.600
- [Victoria] All local
people are valuable experts.

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:20.230
- Here lately we've had
these 65, 70 mile an hour

00:04:20.230 --> 00:04:22.350
Northwest winds, Northeast winds

00:04:22.350 --> 00:04:25.073
and there's nothin' here to stop it.

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:27.680
- [Victoria] The best
knowledge that we get

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:31.213
from any place is the people
who are living there 24/7,

00:04:31.213 --> 00:04:34.440
and the people who are
living through those changes.

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:36.950
- When you got two, almost
two and a half miles

00:04:36.950 --> 00:04:38.283
of water front, it's hard.

00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:42.090
- Especially when that
water front is changing.

00:04:42.090 --> 00:04:42.923
- Constantly.

00:04:43.792 --> 00:04:46.560
(soothing instrumental music)

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:48.730
- [Victoria] I take everything
that I've listened to

00:04:48.730 --> 00:04:52.390
and analyze it, think about
what potential solutions,

00:04:52.390 --> 00:04:54.907
what other expertise we can bring in

00:04:54.907 --> 00:04:57.184
to make that place safe.

00:04:57.184 --> 00:05:01.017
(soothing instrumental music)

00:05:02.020 --> 00:05:04.690
Climate change adaptations
along shore lines

00:05:04.690 --> 00:05:06.950
often take two different forms.

00:05:06.950 --> 00:05:08.380
Greener solutions,

00:05:08.380 --> 00:05:12.240
which use nature to make our
landscapes more resilient,

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:14.440
creating salt resistant marshes,

00:05:14.440 --> 00:05:18.180
creating areas that can
flood and bounce back,

00:05:18.180 --> 00:05:20.060
or more gray solutions,

00:05:20.060 --> 00:05:22.733
using concrete to build up a sea wall.

00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:27.664
Patriot Point can benefit
from both of these.

00:05:27.664 --> 00:05:29.830
(soothing instrumental music)

00:05:29.830 --> 00:05:33.200
If you've grown up working
and living on the water,

00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:35.870
not being able to live
on the Chesapeake Bay

00:05:35.870 --> 00:05:38.180
fundamentally changes who you are,

00:05:38.180 --> 00:05:39.770
and that's what people are afraid of.

00:05:39.770 --> 00:05:42.373
People are afraid of
losing their identity.

00:05:43.804 --> 00:05:44.993
- I love this area.

00:05:47.250 --> 00:05:49.213
Born and raised here, and like I say,

00:05:50.260 --> 00:05:51.950
this is what I truly love.

00:05:51.950 --> 00:05:53.520
- [Victoria] How can you leave this place?

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:55.080
- [Older Man] That's it.

00:05:55.080 --> 00:05:56.960
- [Victoria] Each of us has expertise

00:05:56.960 --> 00:05:59.703
that we need to share so we
can make better solutions.

00:06:01.470 --> 00:06:03.230
We have skills based volunteers

00:06:03.230 --> 00:06:05.150
working all across the country,

00:06:05.150 --> 00:06:08.300
whether it's rebuilding
a sea wall in Alaska,

00:06:08.300 --> 00:06:11.223
helping to move a building in Louisiana,

00:06:12.550 --> 00:06:14.720
we are helping preserve local histories

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:16.333
in the face of sea level rise.

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:20.620
The more people we have

00:06:20.620 --> 00:06:22.800
working on a climate change solution,

00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:24.173
the better it will be.

00:06:25.074 --> 00:06:27.960
(soothing instrumental music)

00:06:27.960 --> 00:06:31.330
My grandparents were Holocaust survivors.

00:06:31.330 --> 00:06:34.500
They were survivors of
Auschwitz concentration camp

00:06:34.500 --> 00:06:38.790
and they came to this country
with nothing but themselves

00:06:38.790 --> 00:06:41.040
and the memories of those who were lost

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:42.570
and built an incredible life

00:06:42.570 --> 00:06:44.253
and gave back to their community.

00:06:45.620 --> 00:06:48.800
If they could find hope and
resilience in that story,

00:06:48.800 --> 00:06:51.760
there's no reason why I
can't share that same hope

00:06:51.760 --> 00:06:54.383
and resilience in climate
change stories today.

00:06:56.700 --> 00:07:00.030
The future can be hopeful
if you can work together

00:07:00.030 --> 00:07:02.456
and you can identify what every person

00:07:02.456 --> 00:07:06.208
can contribute to a
climate change solution.

00:07:06.208 --> 00:07:10.041
(soothing instrumental music)

